they've been called the worst floods in  pakistan's history by the country's  prime minister  one third of the nation's land is under  water  so what has caused these floods to be so  bad and how have people been affected  for the best of sky news videos  subscribe to our youtube channel just  tap the button below  an estimated 33 million people have been  impacted by these floods and over 1  500 people have died each of pakistan's  four provinces have been affected but  the southern states of sindh and  balochistan have been hit the hardest  roads bridges and key infrastructure  have been left underwater  these satellite images show towns and  cities completely submerged  communities have been totally cut off  and left without food and drinking water  but these floods will have lasting  effects to come  many crops have been totally wiped out  including 45 of cotton pakistan's  planning minister says that it will cost  around 10 billion dollars to rebuild the  country now pakistan has experienced  flooding before but not to this extent  so why is it so bad this time  the indus river which runs through the  country from the north in the himalayas  down into the arabian sea is actually  prone to flooding in the months of july  and august  but this is unprecedented and one reason  why is rising temperatures now pakistan  is home to more than 7 000 glaciers more  than any country in the world outside  the north and south poles now these  rising temperatures are causing the  glaciers to melt forming glacial lakes  around 3 000 have already formed and  officials are warning that 33 of them  are close to bursting  a second factor is pakistan's monsoon  season which started earlier than normal  this year it's been dubbed the monster  monsoon this monster is something we  have never seen it typically lasts for  one and a half months in july and august  but the intensity frequency and duration  of rain has been changing this monsoon  season is different this time it's been  unrelenting no break eight weeks monsoon  season should have stopped by end of  july sindh province has had nearly eight  times more rain than normal this year  and balochistan province has had five  times its normal level  pakistan is the eighth most vulnerable  country in the world to extreme weather  and melting glaciers caused by rising  temperatures can be attributed to global  warming in april the mercury and sins  navarb hit a whopping 50 degrees celsius  it is an entirely new level of climate  led catastrophe pakistan has said it's  at the ground zero of climate change  despite their carbon footprint being  less than one percent of global  emissions and the country's planning  minister has said richer countries have  a responsibility to help the nation  because they've caused climate change  pakistan's government has been widely  criticized for ignoring the urgency of  climate change and not building enough  preventative infrastructure in 2010  massive floods killed around 2 000  people but since no plans have been  implemented to prevent future floods and  now newly formed glacial lakes have  become tourist attractions developers  have been given permission to build  hotels on the banks of swollen rivers  with disastrous effects  the floods could hardly have come at a  worse time for a country already in  turmoil pakistan's elected prime  minister imran khan was ousted in april  leading to political turmoil inflation  and employment are high and the repeal  is depreciating  as well as difficulty distributing aid  and reaching cut-off communities there  are now fears of water-borne diseases  spreading  because the water simply isn't draining  away as for climate change the number of  days of predicted rainfall in pakistan  are set to rise massively  even a limit of 1.5 degrees celsius on  global warming by 2050 will still mean a  third of pakistan's glaciers melting  [Music]  you
 

